## Usage

### Dry Run Mode

Preview changes without executing them:

```bash
{{ project_slug }} --dry-run input.txt
```

Dry run mode shows what would happen without making any changes. Output includes:
- Source file information
- Operation details
- Destructive operation warnings (if applicable)
- Status indicating no changes were made

### Destructive Operations

The CLI detects potentially destructive operations and prompts for confirmation before executing them. Destructive operations include:
- File deletion
- Directory deletion
- File overwrites
- Configuration changes
- Database operations

#### Confirmation Prompts

When a destructive operation is detected, you'll see:

```
⚠️  Delete file: important_file.txt [y/N]:
```

#### Bypassing Confirmation

Use the `--force` flag to bypass confirmation prompts:

```bash
{{ project_slug }} --force delete important_file.txt
```

**Note:** Use `--force` with caution, especially in automation scripts.

#### Safe Mode

Dry run mode automatically bypasses confirmation prompts since no changes are made:

```bash
{{ project_slug }} --dry-run delete important_file.txt
```

### Output Formats and Field Selection

The CLI supports multiple output formats optimized for different use cases:

#### Output Formats

- **TOON** (Token-Oriented Object Notation): Token-efficient format for AI agents (~40% savings vs JSON)
- **JSON**: Standard JSON format for machine processing
- **Human**: Human-readable formatted output

Format selection is automatic based on mode:
- Agent mode defaults to TOON format
- Human mode defaults to human-readable output

```bash
# Override format
{{ project_slug }} --format toon input.txt
{{ project_slug }} --format json input.txt
{{ project_slug }} --format human input.txt

# Quick TOON output
{{ project_slug }} --toon input.txt
```

#### Field Selection

In agent mode, the CLI uses minimal default schemas to reduce token consumption. You can select specific fields using the `--fields` flag:

```bash
# Select specific fields (comma-separated)
{{ project_slug }} --fields id,name,status input.txt

# Combine with format selection
{{ project_slug }} --format toon --fields id,name input.txt
```

Default schemas include 3-4 essential fields (identifier, title, status) while excluding long-form content (descriptions, bodies) from list views. Use `--fields` to request additional fields when needed.

### Man Pages

The CLI includes traditional Unix man pages for comprehensive documentation.

#### Viewing Man Pages

You can view the man page in two ways:

```bash
# Using the --man flag
{{ project_slug }} --man

# Using the system man command (after installation)
man {{ project_slug }}
```

#### Installing Man Pages

Man pages are automatically installed when you run the install command:

```bash
{{ project_slug }} --install
```

This installs the man page to `~/.local/share/man/man1/{{ project_slug }}.1` (or `$XDG_DATA_HOME/man/man1/` if set).

#### Generating Man Pages

Man pages are generated from templates during the build process:

```bash
# Generate man pages
just generate-man-pages

# Validate man page format
just validate-man-pages
```

#### Man Page Content

The man page includes:
- Command name and description
- Synopsis with all options
- Detailed description
- Complete list of options and flags
- Examples and usage notes

#### Man Page Sections

The man page includes the following sections:
- **NAME**: Command name and brief description
- **SYNOPSIS**: Command usage syntax
- **DESCRIPTION**: Detailed description of the command
- **OPTIONS**: All available command-line options
- **COMMANDS**: Available subcommands
- **ENVIRONMENT**: Environment variables that affect behavior
- **FILES**: Configuration and data file locations
- **EXAMPLES**: Usage examples
- **EXIT STATUS**: Exit code meanings
- **BUGS**: Where to report bugs
- **SEE ALSO**: Related documentation

## Configuration

The CLI supports multiple configuration sources with the following precedence (highest to lowest):

1. Command-line arguments
2. Environment variables (`{{ PROJECT_SLUG }}_*`)
3. Local config (`.config/{{ project_slug }}/config.toml`)
4. User config (`~/.config/myorg/{{ project_slug }}/config.toml`)
5. System config (`/etc/{{ project_slug }}/config.toml`)
6. Default values

### AXI (Agent eXperience Interface) Configuration

The CLI includes AXI-specific settings for AI agent integration:

```toml
# Mode: agent or human
mode = "agent"

# Default output format for agent mode: toon, json, or human
default_format = "toon"

# Truncation limit for long output fields (characters)
truncation_limit = 1000

# Enable contextual help in agent mode
enable_contextual_help = true

# Enable session context output in agent mode
session_context_enabled = false
```

See [docs/config-precedence.md](docs/config-precedence.md) for detailed configuration documentation.

### Field Selection with --fields

The CLI supports field selection to reduce output size in agent mode. Use the `--fields` flag to specify which fields to include in output:

```bash
# Default output (minimal schema: id, title, status)
{{ project_slug }} list-items

# Select specific fields
{{ project_slug }} list-items --fields id,title,created_at

# Select all available fields
{{ project_slug }} list-items --fields id,title,status,created_at,updated_at,body
```

**Available fields vary by command.** Use `--fields` with an invalid field name to see available fields for the current command.

**Default behavior:**
- Agent mode: Uses minimal default schema (3-4 fields) to reduce token consumption
- Human mode: Uses full schema with all fields
- List outputs: Limited to 100 items by default (configurable via schema)

### Pre-computed Aggregates

The CLI includes pre-computed aggregates to reduce the need for follow-up API calls in agent interactions. Aggregates provide commonly-needed data upfront.

#### Count Fields

List outputs include total count information:

**TOON Format**
```
count: 30 of 847 total
```

**JSON Format**
```json
{
  "count": 30,
  "total": 847
}
```

#### Derived Status Fields

Detail views include lightweight derived status summaries:

**TOON Format**
```
# Derived status:
test_summary: 3/3 passed
comment_count: 7 comments
progress: 75% complete
```

**JSON Format**
```json
{
  "derived_status": {
    "fields": [
      {"name": "test_summary", "value": "3/3 passed", "lightweight": true},
      {"name": "comment_count", "value": "7 comments", "lightweight": true},
      {"name": "progress", "value": "75% complete", "lightweight": true}
    ]
  }
}
```

**Available derived fields:**
- `test_summary` - Test pass/fail status (e.g., "3/3 passed")
- `comment_count` - Number of comments (e.g., "7 comments")
- `progress` - Completion percentage (e.g., "75% complete")

**Performance characteristics:**
- Aggregates are only computed when the backend can provide them cheaply
- Derived fields are lightweight (<10ms computation time)
- Count computation uses backend COUNT queries when available
- Fallback to in-memory counting for backends without COUNT support

## Structured Error Format

The CLI uses structured error formatting to provide consistent, machine-readable errors that AI agents can parse programmatically.

### Error Format Examples

**TOON Format (Agent Mode)**
```
error[1]{code,message}: "MISSING_REQUIRED_FLAG","--title is required"
help[1]: "{{ project_slug }} create --title \"...\""
```

**JSON Format**
```json
{
  "error": {
    "code": "MISSING_REQUIRED_FLAG",
    "message": "--title is required"
  },
  "help": ["{{ project_slug }} create --title \"...\""]
}
```

**Human Format**
```
Error: --title is required

Suggestion: {{ project_slug }} create --title "..."
```

### Error Codes

| Code | Exit Code | Description |
|------|-----------|-------------|
| `MISSING_REQUIRED_FLAG` | 2 | Required flag not provided |
| `INVALID_FLAG_VALUE` | 2 | Flag value doesn't match expected format |
| `CONFLICTING_FLAGS` | 2 | Mutually exclusive flags provided |
| `UNKNOWN_FLAG` | 2 | Unknown flag provided |
| `NOT_FOUND` | 1 | Resource not found |
| `ALREADY_EXISTS` | 1 | Resource already exists |
| `PERMISSION_DENIED` | 1 | Insufficient permissions |
| `NETWORK_ERROR` | 1 | Network operation failed |
| `INTERNAL_ERROR` | 1 | Unexpected internal error |
| `NO_OP` | 0 | Desired state already exists (idempotent) |

### Output Channels

- **stdout**: Structured errors in TOON/JSON/human format
- **stderr**: Diagnostic messages, debug logging, progress indicators

### Key Features

- **Actionable suggestions**: Every error includes at least one next step
- **No raw dependency output**: External tool errors are translated to structured format
- **CLI command references**: Suggestions reference CLI commands, not underlying tools
- **Idempotent operations**: No-op scenarios exit with code 0

## Idempotent Operations

The CLI supports idempotent operations to ensure that repeating commands doesn't cause errors when the desired state already exists.

### Idempotency Behavior

**Create Operations**
- If resource doesn't exist: Create normally
- If resource exists with same state: Return success with no-op acknowledgment (exit code 0)
- If resource exists with different state: Return error with suggestion to update

**Update Operations**
- If resource doesn't exist: Return error (not found)
- If resource exists with same state: Return success with no-op acknowledgment (exit code 0)
- If resource exists with different state: Update normally

**Delete Operations**
- If resource doesn't exist: Return success with no-op acknowledgment (exit code 0)
- If resource exists: Delete normally

**Close Operations**
- If resource doesn't exist: Return error (not found)
- If resource already closed: Return success with no-op acknowledgment (exit code 0)
- If resource open: Close normally

### No-Op Acknowledgment Format

**TOON Format**
```
resource[1]{id,status}: "42","closed"
note[1]: "Task #42 already closed (no-op)"
```

**JSON Format**
```json
{
  "resource": {
    "id": "42",
    "status": "closed"
  },
  "note": "Task #42 already closed (no-op)"
}
```

**Human Format**
```
Task #42 already closed (no-op)
```

### Exit Code Semantics

- **Exit code 0**: Successful operation or successful no-op (desired state already exists)
- **Exit code 1**: Error (resource not found, permission denied, etc.)
- **Exit code 2**: Usage error (invalid input, missing required flag, etc.)

## Prompt Suppression

The CLI automatically suppresses interactive prompts in agent mode to ensure non-interactive operation. Human mode retains prompts for better user experience.

### Mode-Based Prompt Behavior

**Agent Mode**
- All interactive prompts are suppressed
- Missing required values fail immediately with structured error
- Destructive operations auto-confirm (no confirmation prompt)
- Wrapped tools invoked with non-interactive flags

**Human Mode**
- Interactive prompts work normally
- `--force` flag bypasses confirmation prompts
- Missing required values prompt for input
- Wrapped tools invoked normally (unless `--force`)

### --force Flag

The `--force` flag bypasses confirmation prompts in human mode:

```bash
# Without --force (prompts for confirmation)
{{ project_slug }} delete important_file.txt
# Output: ⚠️  This will delete file: important_file.txt [y/N]:

# With --force (auto-confirms)
{{ project_slug }} delete --force important_file.txt
# Output: Deleting file: important_file.txt
```

**Note**: In agent mode, `--force` is implicitly enabled for all operations.

### Missing Required Values

**Agent Mode (fail immediately)**
```bash
{{ project_slug }} create
# Output: error[2]{code,message}: "MISSING_REQUIRED_FLAG","--title is required"
#         help[1]: "{{ project_slug }} create --title \"...\""
# Exit code: 2
```

**Human Mode (prompt for input)**
```bash
{{ project_slug }} create
# Output: Enter title: [prompt for input]
```

## Session Integration

The CLI supports session hooks for ambient context injection in AI agents.

### --session-context Command

Outputs compact session context in TOON format for agent consumption:

```bash
{{ project_slug }} --session-context
```

**Output:**
```
cwd: "/path/to/project"
repo_root: "/path/to/project"
repo_type: "git"
branch: "main"
recent_operations: 5
session_id: "abc123"
```

### --install-agent-hooks Command

Registers session hooks for Claude Code and Codex:

```bash
{{ project_slug }} --install-agent-hooks
```

This command:
- Installs session-start hooks to inject context
- Installs session-end hooks to capture metadata
- Uses portable command resolution (PATH-verified or absolute path)
- Is idempotent (silent no-ops for same path)

**Note**: Hook installation requires write permissions to agent configuration directories.

## Agent Skills

The CLI supports agent skill generation for discoverability via agentskills.io.

### --generate-skill Command

Generates a SKILL.md file for agent discoverability:

```bash
{{ project_slug }} --generate-skill > SKILL.md
```

The generated skill:
- Uses the same content as the no-args home view
- Strips live state (repository-specific paths, environment values)
- Rewrites command examples to non-interactive form
- Includes trigger-shaped frontmatter (name and description)

### --check-skill Command

Validates that the committed SKILL.md is not stale:

```bash
{{ project_slug }} --check-skill
```

This command is intended for CI/CD pipelines and fails if the skill content differs from the current CLI implementation.

## Integration Methods

You can integrate the CLI with AI agents using two methods:

### 1. Session Hooks (Recommended for Local Development)

Install session hooks for automatic context injection:

```bash
{{ project_slug }} --install-agent-hooks
```

**Pros**: Automatic context injection, no manual configuration
**Cons**: Requires hook installation, agent-specific configuration

### 2. Agent Skills (Recommended for Cloud/Remote)

Generate and publish SKILL.md for agent discoverability:

```bash
{{ project_slug }} --generate-skill > SKILL.md
```

**Pros**: No installation required, works with agentskills.io
**Cons**: Manual regeneration when CLI changes

**Note**: You only need one integration method - choose based on your use case.

## No-Args Behavior

Running the CLI with no arguments shows the most relevant live content, not a usage manual. This enables agents to see actual state immediately and act without a second call.

**Agent Mode (TOON format)**
```
items[5]{id,title,status}: "1","Task 1","open","2","Task 2","in-progress","3","Task 3","closed","4","Task 4","open","5","Task 5","open"
count: 5 of 10 total
recent_operations: 3
```

**Human Mode (Human-readable format)**
```
Current State:
- 5 items total (3 open, 1 in-progress, 1 closed)
- Recent operations: 3
- Repository: main branch

Suggestions:
- {{ project_slug }} list --toon
- {{ project_slug }} create --title "..."
```

**Detailed Help**

For detailed usage information, use the `--help` flag:

```bash
{{ project_slug }} --help
```

**Example:**
```bash
# Agent mode with default fields (minimal output)
{{ project_slug }} --toon list-items
# Output: id,title,status for each item

# Agent mode with custom fields
{{ project_slug }} --toon list-items --fields id,title,created_at
# Output: id,title,created_at for each item

# Human mode with JSON output
{{ project_slug }} --json list-items
# Output: All fields in JSON format
```

## Development

This project uses **devbox** for environment management and **just** for task running.

### Prerequisites

- [devbox](https://www.jetify.com/devbox)
- [just](https://github.com/casey/just)

### Setup

```bash
# Enter the devbox shell
devbox shell

# Or run commands directly
devbox run just build
```

### Common Commands

```bash
just build          # Build the project
just test           # Run tests
just lint           # Run clippy lints
just quality        # Run all quality checks (lint, test, typecheck, audit, outdated)
just validate       # Full validation (fmt, lint, test, doc, audit, outdated)
just audit          # Audit dependencies for security vulnerabilities
just outdated       # Check for available dependency updates
just release        # Full release pipeline (includes audit + outdated)
just doctor         # Check environment health
```

